course: dictionary
lesson: 3164
title: Emoticons: faces made from keys
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An emoticon is a small face made from keyboard symbols, like :-) or :(. Read it sideways: the colon is the eyes, the dash is the nose, and the bracket is the smile or the frown. People add emoticons to messages to show a feeling, so the reader knows the mood behind the words.
explain: «Emoticons are faces made from keyboard characters, like :-) and :-(» Informal writers also call a picture face an emoji. Context helps you work out the meaning: in «Sorry I was late :-(», the sad face shows the writer feels sorry.
explain: Emoticons can show if someone is joking, but they can also be misunderstood. A smiley emoticon might look friendly to one reader and sarcastic to another. Before you add one, ask yourself: will the reader understand the feeling I mean?
fact: An emoticon is a small face made from keyboard symbols.
fact: :-) is a smiley face, and :-( is a sad face.
fact: The plural is «emoticons». The singular is «an emoticon».
fact: The computer scientist Scott Fahlman suggested emoticons in 1982.
fact: Informally, an emoji is a little picture showing a face or thing.
exercise: What is an emoticon? | A large picture printed on paper | *A small face made from keyboard symbols | A computer that sends messages | A type of computer keyboard
exercise: «Sorry I was late :-(» What feeling does the emoticon show? | Joy at a party | Anger at a bus driver | *Sadness or sorry | Boredom at school
exercise: Which emoticon shows a smile? | *:-) | :-O | :-| | :-(
test: Why might a smiley emoticon be misunderstood? | Because it always means anger | Because it cannot be typed on a keyboard | *Because one reader may take it as friendly and another as sarcastic | Because it is too long to read
test: Who suggested emoticons in 1982? | A famous footballer | Queen Victoria | *Scott Fahlman | A teacher in Glasgow
exam: Which word means a little picture showing a face or thing, used informally? | Emotion | *Emoji | Keyboard | Emoticon
